[{:adr/repos "kotoba-lang/kotoba-native, kotoba-lang/kotoba-verifier, kotoba-lang/osaho, kotoba-lang/amu", :adr/related "kotoba-native ADR 0081, kotoba-native ADR 0002, kotoba-verifier ADR 0051, osaho ADR 0271, adr-2607198300-kotoba-execution-without-jvm-node-rust", :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2609093000-a-command-needs-stdout-before-it-needs-a-repo", :adr/body "Owner instruction, 2026-09-09: build kotoba/amu native binaries that run as fast\nas Rust, one repo per command, in kotoba-lang. This ADR records what was\nMEASURED that day before any repo was created, because the request assumes a\nnative command binary is reachable today and three quarters of it is not.\n\n## What runs today (measured 2026-09-09, aarch64-macos, amu --jvm-free)\n\nReproduction, not values -- the values move:\n\n    A=orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/bin/amu\n    printf '(ns t (:export [main]))\\n(defn main [] :i64 ...)\\n' > /tmp/t.kotoba\n    $A compile /tmp/t.kotoba --jvm-free --target aarch64-macos --output /tmp/t.kexe\n    $A extract-native /tmp/t.kexe --symbol main --output /tmp/m.bin\n    cc -O2 -o /tmp/loader orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/tools/kexe_loader.c\n    /tmp/loader /tmp/m.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 <allow-csv|->\n\n| face | state |\n|---|---|\n| .kotoba to machine code, executed | works |\n| fs/browse (wire 34), directory listing, executed | works |\n| fs/app-data (wire 35) read / write / ranged read | hosted by the loader |\n| env/read (wire 33) | hosted by the loader |\n| string-substring / string-byte-length / string-contains? | lower to native |\n| string-index-of | REFUSED, see below |\n| stdout, argv, exit status | wires 37, 38, 39 -- added 2026-09-10 |\n| standalone executable | scripts/package-command.cljs -- added 2026-09-10 |\n\nThe last two rows are what stands between this and a command. The loader is a\nHOST: it mmaps the code, answers capability calls, and prints the RETURN VALUE\nas an EDN map. There is no way for a guest to write a byte to stdout, read its\nown argv, or set an exit status, and `kotoba.native.macho` encodes a `.o`\nobject, not a linked executable. `capability-catalog.edn` lists 35 capabilities\nand none of them is stdout or argv -- so this is not a qualification gap in a\nkit, it is an absent capability.\n\n## The first blocker, and why it was the right one to take first\n\n`string-index-of` failed as `aggregate ABI rejected: call-abi-not-admitted`.\nWithout an offset a guest cannot cut a string at a separator, so it cannot\nsplit lines -- which removes grep, wc, cut, head, tail, sort and uniq at once,\nand is why `kbb.browse` could not compile for native even though the loader has\nhosted its provider all along.\n\nIt turned out to be one line of exposure, not a feature: `kotoba$string-find`,\nthe helper kotoba-native ADR 0002 already had, ALREADY answers the first\nmatching byte offset. `string-contains?` was folding that offset to 0/1 and\nthrowing it away.\n\nLanded as three changes, because there are three gates in front of it and ADR\n0002 measured that opening one alone moves nothing:\n\n  * kotoba-native `lower-index-of` + dispatch on both ISAs (its ADR 0081)\n  * kotoba-verifier `string-operations` gains `string-index-of 2` (its ADR 0051)\n  * osaho: the oracle itself was wrong (its ADR 0271)\n\n## The reference interpreter was the implementation that was wrong\n\nkotoba-native's string-search suite never writes an expected value down: each\nrow runs through `kotoba.kir/execute` twice, once as the operation and once as\nthe rewrite. Adding an astral row made that suite fail as `(not (= 7 4))` on\n`(string-index-of` G-clef + `ab, ab)`.\n\n`kotoba.kir.value/utf8-index-of!` walked the haystack one UTF-16 unit at a\ntime, charged a high surrogate 4 bytes, then stepped onto the LOW surrogate and\ncharged 3 more. Every astral code point before a match added 7. The JS emitter,\nthe new native lowering and CPython all answer 4.\n\nNothing had caught it because every multi-byte test row used 2- and 3-byte code\npoints, which are one UTF-16 unit each. The existing test even named the wrong\nanswer it was guarding against -- a UTF-16 answer of 6 -- and 7 is neither that\nnor the right one. It also reached artifacts, not just runtime: `lower` folds a\npure i64 entry through this evaluator, so a constant `string-index-of` over an\nastral literal was baked in wrong.\n\nThis is the workspace rule about oracles paying for itself. A suite that had\nwritten 4 down would have been GREEN and the reference would still be wrong.\n\n## Decisions\n\n1. Foundation before repos, each step proved by EXECUTING the artifact rather\n   than by building it. The remaining foundation is: argv, stdout/stderr and\n   exit status as capabilities with loader providers, then a linked executable\n   so `./ls` is a file rather than a blob plus a loader invocation.\n2. Naming is the ORIGIN plane. POSIX utilities are specified by IEEE Std\n   1003.1, so they take `org-ieee-<utility>`, following `org-ieee-tar`, which\n   states that pattern for exactly this reason. Non-POSIX entries in the\n   owner's list route to their own origin instead -- ssh is IETF and\n   `org-ietf-ssh` already exists.\n3. The command set is the 28 the owner listed. `tar` is already\n   `org-ieee-tar` (a format library, not yet a command surface); awk, sed,\n   curl and ssh are each a larger subject than the rest and are sequenced last.\n\n## Not done, and named so it is not mistaken for done\n\nNo command repo exists yet, and none can produce a runnable binary until argv,\nstdout and exit status exist. Do not scaffold 28 repos against a runtime that\ncannot print. The next step is the loader's provider table, not a repo.\n\n\n## Where this stands (2026-09-10)\n\nTwo rows in the table above read `do not exist` when this was written and\ndo not now. They were changed in place rather than left standing: a reader\nreaching that table needs the current position, and the history of why is\nin the prose around it.\n\n### The foundation decision 1 asked for is delivered\n\nThree capability wires, each with a loader provider, each carried through\nthe whole catalog chain -- kotoba-lang authority, kotoba-sema frontend map,\namu loader -- because a capability in the authority is invisible to the\ncompiler until the sema pin moves:\n\n    :io/write        37   bytes to fd 1, answered with the decimal count\n    :cli/args        38   empty request -> count, decimal -> that argument\n    :io/write-error  39   the same for fd 2\n\n:fs/app-data (35) also gained an EXISTS form, which is what lets a command\ntell `no such file` from `empty file` without trapping. Out of scope it\nanswers 0 rather than refusing, so probing cannot map the filesystem\noutside the grant.\n\nscripts/package-command.cljs turns an extracted blob into a standalone\nMach-O, so a command is a file rather than a loader invocation. Grant,\nfilesystem scope, browse scope, string arena, fuel and pairs are baked in\nat package time and are NOT movable by the caller.\n\n### The ceilings found by writing the commands\n\nEach measured rather than read off a document, and each shaped code:\n\n  * Five parameters. kotoba.compiler.frontend/max-parameters is 5, an ABI\n    arity limit rather than a language decision. head and tail pack an exit\n    status and a header flag into one word; wc computes its totals in a\n    SECOND walk, because three sums plus index, count and flag is seven.\n  * The wire carries UTF-8, not bytes. Every string result is validated as\n    canonical UTF-8, so cp cannot copy a file that is not -- it traps rather\n    than truncating. cp is otherwise a byte copier, so that is a real\n    restriction of domain and its README names it.\n  * No stdin, and no cwd. Every command refuses rather than pretending to\n    have read an empty stdin. pwd was deliberately NOT shipped: /bin/pwd -L\n    validates PWD against the real directory -- with PWD=/tmp it prints the\n    physical path anyway -- which needs getcwd. EXISTS cannot substitute,\n    since /tmp exists and is still the wrong answer. That is a :sys/cwd\n    wire, not a command.\n\n### Repos\n\nTwelve of the 28, each with a suite comparing stdout, stderr AND exit status\nagainst the system utility, and negative controls that fail for the reason\nthey name: echo, cat, ls, wc, head, grep, sort, uniq, tail, cut, find, cp.\n\nThe rest are capability-blocked and the blocker is nameable in each case:\nfs create/delete/rename for mkdir, mv, rm; process spawn for xargs, ps,\nkill; syscalls for df, du, free, chmod, chown; network for curl. awk and\nsed are larger subjects, as decision 3 says.\n\n### What the suites caught, which is the point of writing them\n\nFour cases in one day where a test could not have failed: three harnesses\nthat dropped every operand past the first or second (grep, sort, and the\necho suite through zsh word splitting), and one naming a fixture that did\nnot exist, so both implementations agreed on the same missing-file error.\nEach was visible in the OUTPUT rather than the status -- prefixes that never\nappeared, lines from a file that was never read.\n\nThey also caught two real defects in the commands they were added for: cut\nterminated an undelimited last line that /usr/bin/cut leaves unterminated,\na single-operand bug only two operands made visible; and uniq keyed its -c\nflag off the argument COUNT, which stops being right the moment a second\noperand exists.\n\nThe loader identity is the other thing measured here. kototama-native\nhashes the reviewed loader source and refuses a mismatch, so changing\nkexe_loader.c without advancing kotoba-lang/artifact fails EVERY native\nexecution -- 25 failures and 109 errors in one suite, all one cause. Its\nsupervisor report compares key sets for exact equality, so the posix and\nwindows loaders must agree on which keys a report has or one of them is\nrejected as malformed evidence.", :adr/date "2026-09-09", :db/id -1, :adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-09", :adr/title "A command needs stdout before it needs a repo, and the scan already knew the offset"}]
